<p>An objection is usually a question in disguise: “help me feel sure about this”. Don’t argue with it. Find out what’s behind it.</p>

<h2>Listen, acknowledge, explore, respond</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Listen</strong> all the way to the end.</li>
  <li><strong>Acknowledge</strong> it’s reasonable: <em>“That makes sense.”</em></li>
  <li><strong>Explore</strong> with a question: <em>“Can I ask what’s behind that?”</em></li>
  <li><strong>Respond</strong> to the real concern, using what they told you matters to them, then check: <em>“Does that help?”</em></li>
</ol>

<h2>Common ones</h2>
<dl>
  <dt>“It’s too expensive.”</dt>
  <dd>Ask what they’re comparing it with, or whether it’s the total or how it’s paid. Tie the price to the result they said they wanted. Offer a smaller first step before a discount.</dd>
  <dt>“Now’s not a good time.”</dt>
  <dd><em>“Understood. What would need to happen for it to be the right time?”</em> Agree when to check back, and set that date as the next step.</dd>
  <dt>“I need to think about it.”</dt>
  <dd><em>“Of course. So I can help, what part are you weighing up?”</em> Then set the next step before the call ends.</dd>
  <dt>“We’re talking to others.”</dt>
  <dd><em>“That’s sensible. What will help you decide between us?”</em> Then make sure you’ve shown that.</dd>
  <dt>“Just send me some information.”</dt>
  <dd>Send it, and ask one question that keeps the conversation going: <em>“So I send the right thing, is it mostly about … or …?”</em></dd>
</dl>
<p>The <em>“It’s too expensive”</em> and <em>“I need to think about it”</em> call templates have these ready to say.</p>
